.Dd October 9, 2026
.Dt NAMEFS 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm namefs
.Nd publish a Door capability at a filesystem pathname
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Cd "options DOORFS"
.Cd "options NAMEFS"
.In door.h
.Ft int
.Fn fattach "int doorfd" "const char *path"
.Ft int
.Fn fdetach "const char *path"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a file-mount filesystem used by
.Xr fattach 3
to publish a live Door descriptor at an existing regular-file pathname.
It does not provide a separately mountable namespace and anonymous mounts are
not supported.
.Pp
Opening a live publication atomically replaces the provisional vnode file with
a native Door client capability.
A successful
.Xr open 2
therefore always selects and pins one live Door generation.
If the publication is in recovery, its target is absent, the Door is revoked,
or jail policy denies the caller, open fails without returning an ordinary
vnode descriptor.
.Ss Attach, detach, and rebind
.Xr fattach 3
creates a publication.
Attaching to an existing publication performs a rebind: new opens select the
new generation while capabilities opened earlier remain pinned to their old
generation.
An already revoked or orphaning Door is rejected.
.Pp
.Xr fdetach 3
validates that the pathname resolves to namefs and unmounts the exact
filesystem ID.
Detaching removes discovery through the pathname but does not invalidate
already-open Door capabilities.
An active publication point cannot be unlinked or renamed.
.Ss Owner exit and recovery
Owner exit revokes the Door immediately and schedules bounded namefs GC
probes across publications. A revoked target can reject open before the mount
state changes. GC enters RECOVERY when only namefs holds the target file
reference; inherited or duplicated target references can delay that transition.
External vnode references are a separate criterion: they keep the publication
available for rebind. An orphaned mount without those references can be
unmounted asynchronously. Rebind resets the target and probe budget; a failed
unmount restores RECOVERY.
.Ss Nullfs stacking
Publishing namefs on a nullfs vnode is supported.
Detachment uses the validated filesystem ID so it does not depend on ambiguous
pathname reconstruction through the stack.
.Pp
Mounting nullfs above a directory that already contains a nested namefs file
mount follows normal nullfs semantics: the nullfs view exposes the covered
placeholder, not the nested namefs mount.
The placeholder is not a Door and Door operations on it fail.
The original namefs pathname remains usable.
.Ss Jails
A same-prison open is allowed subject to normal mode checks.
A host publication is hidden from a jailed opener unless its Door was created
with
.Dv DOOR_CROSS_JAIL .
The policy is checked when open creates the descriptor capability.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The target is not suitable for a namefs file mount, the descriptor is not a
Door, or detach does not resolve to namefs.
.It Bq Er EBADF
The Door is revoked, orphaning, or otherwise not live.
.It Bq Er ENXIO
The publication has no live target or is in recovery.
.It Bq Er EACCES
Mode or jail policy denies capability acquisition.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
The pathname is already mounted in an incompatible way or the mount is busy.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr door 3 ,
.Xr fattach 3 ,
.Xr fdetach 3 ,
.Xr doorfs 4 ,
.Xr nullfs 4 ,
.Xr jail 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
first appeared in this FreeBSD Doors implementation.
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
The pathname grants discovery, not ambient authority beyond normal open
checks.
After open succeeds, the resulting descriptor is the authority and remains
valid across detach or rebind until revoked or closed.
